{"boundary_checks":5,"check_overhead_seconds":1.3867840766906738,"end_reason":"natural_end","id":"qav2_0863_r2","mode":"controller","n_rollbacks":3,"n_tokens":319,"n_words_approx":249,"p_safe":[2.630744810690832e-06,1.9776799798543226e-06,0.0010370408247369139,0.35694099612808,0.24530516860290774],"question":"Explain how babies are born unable to walk works.","stopped_early_by_probe":false,"t_end":1794387910.719416,"t_start":1794387830.531099,"text":"Babies are born with their eyes open and are able to see, hear, and respond to their environment. However, they are not born with the ability to walk. In fact, babies are not even able to walk until they are around 12-14 months old.\n\nAt birth, babies are relatively helpless and rely on others for support and care. They are able to move their arms and legs, but they are not yet able to coordinate their movements to walk on their own.\n\nHere's what happens during the first few months of life:\n\n* Around 6-8 weeks old, babies start to develop their neck and back muscles, which help them to lift their head and shoulders.\n* By 10-12 weeks old, babies start to develop their leg muscles, but they are still not able to walk.\n* By 12-14 weeks old, babies start to take their first steps, but they are not yet able to walk independently.\n* By 16-18 weeks old, babies start to take more confident steps, but they still need support and guidance to walk.\n* By 20-24 weeks old, babies are able to take more independent steps, but they still need to be carried or held by others to walk.\n\nIt's not until around 12-18 months old that babies are able to walk independently, and it's not until around 2-3 years old that they are able to walk without any support.\n\nSo, to summarize, babies are not born with the ability to walk, but rather develop their motor skills over time through practice and support.","wall_seconds":80.188316822052}
{"boundary_checks":7,"check_overhead_seconds":0.9310803413391113,"end_reason":"natural_end","id":"qav2_0463_r2","mode":"controller","n_rollbacks":2,"n_tokens":459,"n_words_approx":392,"p_safe":[3.6212072490250494e-06,0.003876374061616557,0.8150645836276179,0.9754324173093194,0.6816148000644394,0.6479525587439374,0.9762791009249135],"question":"What is the reason behind radio waves can carry sound?","stopped_early_by_probe":false,"t_end":1794388024.311233,"t_start":1794387910.719806,"text":"Radio waves are a form of electromagnetic radiation that can carry sound because they are a type of wave that can propagate through a medium, such as air, water, or solids. The reason they can carry sound is due to the way they interact with the physical properties of the medium.\n\nIn the case of sound waves, they are a type of mechanical wave that requires a physical medium to propagate. Sound waves are created by the vibration of particles in the medium, such as molecules or atoms, which transfer energy from one particle to another. When these particles vibrate, they create a disturbance that travels through the medium at a specific speed, which is determined by the properties of the medium.\n\nIn the case of sound waves, the medium is the air, which is a gas that is made up of molecules that are in constant motion. When sound waves are generated, they cause the molecules in the air to vibrate back and forth, creating a series of compressions and rarefactions (expansions) that travel through the air. These compressions and rarefactions are what we perceive as sound waves.\n\nThe reason that radio waves can carry sound is that they are a type of electromagnetic wave, which is a type of wave that requires a physical medium to propagate. Electromagnetic waves, including radio waves, have a specific frequency and wavelength that determines their speed and ability to propagate through a medium. The speed of electromagnetic waves is determined by the properties of the medium, such as its density and permittivity.\n\nIn the case of radio waves, the frequency of the wave is typically in the range of 3 kHz to 300 GHz, which is much higher than the frequency of sound waves, which is typically in the range of 20 Hz to 20 kHz. This means that radio waves can travel through a much larger range of frequencies than sound waves, and can penetrate solid objects more easily.\n\nAdditionally, radio waves can also be modulated to carry sound, which means that they can be changed in frequency or amplitude to encode information. This is why radio broadcasting is used to transmit audio and video signals over long distances, and why radio waves can carry a wide range of sounds, from music to voice to weather forecasts.\n\nI hope this helps clarify the reason behind radio waves carrying sound!","wall_seconds":113.59142708778381}
