{"boundary_checks":2,"check_overhead_seconds":0.44536280632019043,"end_reason":"probe_stop","id":"qav2_0345_r3","mode":"controller","n_rollbacks":1,"n_tokens":123,"n_words_approx":92,"p_safe":[0.0047263905783717685,0.9995494265717095],"question":"What is the reason behind coffee tastes bitter?","stopped_early_by_probe":true,"t_end":1794395194.5912,"t_start":1794395161.831859,"text":"Coffee tastes bitter because of the way it interacts with the taste buds on the tongue and the way our brains process the flavors. Here's a simplified explanation:\n\n**The bitter taste receptors:**\n\nCoffee contains a group of compounds called polyphenols, which are responsible for its bitterness. These compounds are called bitter taste receptors, and they're found in the taste buds on the tongue.\n\n**The bitter taste receptors:**\n\nThere are two types of bitter taste receptors:\n\n1. **T1 receptors:** These receptors are responsible for detecting bitter compounds, such as caffeine, quinine, and other bitter compounds found in coffee.","wall_seconds":32.75934100151062}
{"boundary_checks":5,"check_overhead_seconds":0.885174036026001,"end_reason":"natural_end","id":"qav2_0488_r3","mode":"controller","n_rollbacks":2,"n_tokens":265,"n_words_approx":190,"p_safe":[0.00010346609447448528,0.6398639089417643,0.6225439327095302,0.5530024899863498,0.41843175633382657],"question":"Who invented the printing press?","stopped_early_by_probe":false,"t_end":1794395259.887153,"t_start":1794395194.591505,"text":"The invention of the printing press is a bit complex, as it involved the contributions of several individuals over time. However, I can give you a brief overview of the key figures involved.\n\nThe first movable-type printing press was invented by Johannes Gutenberg in the 15th century. Gutenberg, a German goldsmith and printer, is credited with creating the first practical and commercially viable printing press around 1440. His design used a combination of metal type pieces, ink, and a screw-based press to print movable type, making it possible to mass-produce books and other printed materials.\n\nHowever, the development of the printing press was a gradual process that involved the contributions of many other individuals over the centuries. Some notable figures include:\n\n* Johannes Gutenberg's contemporaries, such as Johannes Peto (c. 1390-1453), who also developed a similar printing press.\n* Johannes Gutenberg's apprentice, Peter Schoeffer (c. 1400-1470), who helped refine Gutenberg's design.\n* Johannes Gutenberg's contemporaries, such as Johannes Truchet (c. 1400-1470), who also worked on printing technology.\n\nSo, while Gutenberg is often credited with the invention of the printing press, it's clear that the development of the technology involved the contributions of many individuals over time.","wall_seconds":65.29564785957336}
