{"boundary_checks":2,"check_overhead_seconds":0.0029189586639404297,"end_reason":"probe_stop","id":"qav2_0120_r3","mode":"controller","n_rollbacks":0,"n_tokens":147,"n_words_approx":104,"p_safe":[0.8490701783475949,0.9923060934042051],"question":"Can you explain what causes touchscreens responding to fingers?","stopped_early_by_probe":true,"t_end":1794397718.930613,"t_start":1794397680.059073,"text":"Touchscreens, also known as capacitive touchscreens, respond to touch by detecting changes in capacitance between the screen and the user's fingers. Here's a simplified explanation:\n\n**The Basics**\n\nA touchscreen is made up of a layer of conductive material, usually a thin layer of indium tin oxide (ITO) or a similar material, on top of a substrate. When a user touches the screen with their finger, the finger creates a conductive path between the screen and the ITO layer.\n\n**How it works**\n\nHere's what happens when you touch the screen:\n\n1. **Capacitance**: When you touch the screen, your finger creates a conductive path between the screen and the ITO layer.","wall_seconds":38.87154006958008}
